 so  that already sort of tells you that he's  developed his ability to talk about  science in an engaging way and he taught  my quantum class and after that i was  completely hooked i felt like i have to  be thinking about quantum mechanics and  quantum physics for the rest of my life  after taking his class and so i had a  research experience with him so i was  fortunate to get in his lab i had a  summer research experience funded by the  nsf in their research experience for  undergraduate program where i got to go  to uchicago for a summer and do some  quantum related research um i did an  honors senior a senior honors thesis  where i got to do other kind of  materials quantum research and so when  it was time for me to go to grad school  i was completely sold that i was going  to do some sort of material physics  thing  um but  uh in my process of searching for a  potential phd advisor in grad school i  came across jack harris who was my phd  advisor and  um  i thought his work was incredible that  was incredibly interesting i think you  know when you come in as a graduate  student you're really wide-eyed and you  think everything is amazing and it's  because everything is right yale faculty  and other faculty elsewhere do lots of  very interesting science but i was  convinced that uh the person who became  my phd advisor's work was incredibly  interesting now a funny thing here is  that uh i ended up working on something  completely different in his group right  i had this opportunity to switch away  from doing material physics stuff to  thinking about this you know uh light  interacting with matter thinking about  quantum mechanics thinking about how big  a quantum system can be which like  really  fulfilled my desire to think about  foundations of physics like deep deep  things about why things are the way they  are and what they can be  um and so that's sort of what set me on  the quantum physics trajectory  i love that combination of sort of  breadth and depth that as you sort of  continue going for the fundamentals you  were also sort of  trying out all of these different ways  in which they could come to bear  that's great  um so  we've sort of touched on this broadly  but there's a question that's very  specific  to physics and it's what are some of the  challenges in your opinion that black  underrepresented students and scientists  face in navigating the field  so thinking really specifically about  physics  are there particular things that you  think um black students and scientists  are facing  yeah i think one of the biggest things  is perceptions of ability  so  um unfortunately  there you know many aspects of society  tend to want to force black people in  particular into a certain box  and that box does not include attributes  like smart  and  that  that is a very real thing that you feel  immediately when you walk into an  environment like yale where intelligence  is sort of a currency right well that's  one of the main ways by which you  evaluate a peer or  a near peer or  someone you either outrank or who  outranks you  and so  certainly  perceived intelligence or perceived  incompetence is a is a difficulty  it also turns out that  race class social economic status are  sort of all wound up  uh in the united states  and it also turns out that  that can mean that for some students  some underrepresented some black  students  they could come up in an educational  system that lacked important resources  um and so  i think what that means is  sometimes  i want to be really careful about how i  say this  i think that means that sometimes  students can  uh  find that they have different  preparation than other students and  sometimes they're not in teaching  environments where  the teaching is sort of  cognizant of that fact  right so sometimes you end up in a  classroom where students have different  preparations it's not always the case  that the teaching is geared towards sort  of meeting people where they are and  building them up sometimes teaching can  speak to only the students who have had  the most resources that come from the  most well-funded school districts right  that have the wealthy people who can pay  the taxes which then go and fund the  schools that give some students all  these extra resources compared to other  students and that leads to differences  in in preparation  when going to next steps  um that's an issue that's a sort of  bigger one that's a society-wide that's  a funding that's a how do we  you know  yeah that that that's a tough one but  that's important um and then again i've  already mentioned but  but community right again it's easy to  come into a situation where you were the  only person right for  over a decade i have almost always been  the only person like me in the room and  it's really hard to  have that identity sometimes because i  can tell you when i was a younger grad  student i was deathly afraid of making a  mistake and convincing everyone around  me that  what people thought about black people  was true  that  if i  was to fail a test for example it would  only suggest that it is indeed the case  that black people cannot be physicists  because you don't see them right and  people already sort of hold these  notions sometimes about us  and so  black students or  lgbtq students or  students with various disabilities can  sometimes carry this weight where they  don't see role models people already  don't think that they belong in the  spaces and it's really hard for them to  feel like they can actually be human and  make mistakes without confirming that  they indeed do not belong  and so that is a huge challenge that you  sort of always face  uh when you're under represented student  um i think maybe i'll stop there i can  keep on going but i should stop  no i definitely  i i appreciate that and the ways in  which we have this complicated  um  situation in graduate school where  there's there is this sort of  the way in which your intelligence  perceived is not always accurate and  then there's also imposter syndrome on  top of that and how those two things  play with one another  and that pressure that so many  underrepresented students feel to  actually be the best right um and all of  that combined creates this stressful  environment so often it's um i really  appreciate you speaking about it so  honestly  um so i think we'll jaw i see put a  question in the chat so maybe we'll  we'll use that as our final question um  which is what is your next move uh  academia industry non-profit are you  thinking about this how are you thinking  about this i think this is a question  that our students are constantly  wondering themselves  yeah so i'm thinking about this a lot  and i've been thinking about it for a  long time um  academia all the way so  um i'm positive that i want to be a  professional physicist i can do so in  academia um i also think that  the university system globally uh is  incredibly important i think it's one of  the most important things that humanity  has done and i really want to take a  part in  being a thought leader in physics and  quantum science but also being part of  the leadership of the university system  again shepherding it into the future  making sure that  uh  the university continues to be a like a  hotbed for the production of knowledge  right this is this is important for just  how we understand our place in the  universe as humans but then also for how  we say produce technology that goes and  enriches lives  and i think it's important for us to do  so in a way that's equitable that is not  uh like increasing or driving  disparities in different people's  experiences doing so in a way that  levels the playing field that enriches  people's lives and i want to take a part  in like making sure the university that  i'm at in the future as a faculty member  and that it uh does that and i want to  sort of take the  the role as a leadership as a uh yeah  the role of a leader and sort of be a  role model as a faculty member maybe for  other faculty members uh regarding  what being an effective faculty member  looks like right i don't believe that  that is just being excellent in science  as a physicist i think it's being  excellent at science  being a good teacher  being a good human that cares about  uh  making sure that  different people from different  backgrounds  uh are in a  you know inclusive space that respects  them as people  that  really provides them with the resources  to to thrive  um so yeah  i'm gonna be a professor and i'm looking  forward to it  it's a fantastic answer thank you so  much and we we need you so uh we  appreciate that that path ahead of you  thank you um i want to thank uh dean  cooley i want to thank charles and ja  for their remarks and this conversation  tonight um and also to all the students  and alumni who joined us thank you for  being here um thanks also to my  colleagues yasmina bashirovich regan and  kathleen gallo for all their hard work  in putting this event together along  with the gsa  um i hope everyone has enjoyed the  conversation and will continue to help  us build a stronger community among  graduate students faculty and alumni so  thank you again especially charles thank  you  um we hope that you'll all join us for  our next event in the perspective series  with lindsay mcmillan-brown  name you might have heard earlier today  who's also class of 2019 phd in chemical  engineering who's working as a research  engineer at the nasa glenn research  center in cleveland and that  conversation will take place on december  8th at 8pm more info will be sent out by  email  i also encourage anyone who has not  already done so to join cross campus or  to log in if you haven't done so in a  little while  across campus as you may know is a  fantastic way for alumni and students to  connect with one another formally or  informally  and if there are other ways in which  you'd like to b